 into the context of what Virgil himself concedes is the dimly remembered world that Livy attempts to reconstruct in his history. Most of the cast of characters the poet arrays in support of, and in opposition to, Aeneas as he attempts to gain his promised kingdom would have been almost as unfamiliar to ancient as to modern readers. Virgil's reconstructions, Mezentius, for example, are often very different from representation in other ancient accounts, such as that of Dionysius of Halicarnassus. Others are largely his inventions. The most familiar holdover from the Homeric tradition is Diomedes, Aeneas' opponent in Iliad 5, and the first Greek warrior named by Aeneas himself in Virgil's epic, who is approached by Aeneas' delegates, but eventually decides not to join the battle. This seminar will explore what the last six books tell us of Virgil's design for the epic as a whole.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "Historians, literary critics, and students of Roman philosophy or pre-Roman Italy welcome.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: graduate students.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "GR", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 368159, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "LATIN", "catalogNbr": "7452", "titleShort": "Latin Comparative Grammar", "titleLong": "Latin Comparative Grammar", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 18546, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "07:30PM", "timeEnd": "08:45PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "ins